ld64 253.9, native on the T4240 port only: write the output with write(2)

On this port's kernel, stores through a MAP_SHARED file mapping never reach
the file (exit, munmap and msync all leave zeros).
ld64's Darwin-hosted path ftruncate()s a temp file, mmap()s it shared,
writes the image into the mapping and renames it into place, so every
output whose directory is on HFS came out the right size and all zeros --
measured 2026-11-06 natively building ninja on a T4240: b/ninja, 379596
bytes, hexdump all 00, "cannot execute binary file". A bare output name
skipped the mmap path only by accident (no '/' in it, so ld64 never
statfs()es a directory).

This forces the calloc + write(2) path unless LD_USE_MMAP is set, which is
also how to verify the kernel fix with the same ld. Applied only by
darwin-cross/build-cctools-native.sh: the cross ld on Linux never compiles
the mmap path (#ifdef __APPLE__). Drop it once the kernel is fixed.

--- a/cctools/ld64/src/ld/OutputFile.cpp
+++ b/cctools/ld64/src/ld/OutputFile.cpp
@@ -2872,6 +2872,16 @@
 		}
 	}
 	
+	// patch 0015: on the T4240's Leopard kernel, stores through a
+	// MAP_SHARED file mapping never reach the file, so
+	// every output written below through mmap came out as zeros. Write with
+	// write(2) instead; LD_USE_MMAP=1 restores the mmap path, which is how
+	// to check the kernel fix with this same ld.
+	if ( outputIsMappableFile && getenv("LD_USE_MMAP") == NULL ) {
+		outputIsMappableFile = false;
+		(void)unlink(_options.outputFilePath());	// the write path opens without O_TRUNC
+	}
+
 	//fprintf(stderr, "outputIsMappableFile=%d, outputIsRegularFile=%d, path=%s\n", outputIsMappableFile, outputIsRegularFile, _options.outputFilePath());
 	
 	int fd;
